//- 0:00 TITLE, then 0:15 COLOR DOESN'T EXIST: the thesis, said plainly. Straight in: no hello, no who-am-I. Everything after this is evidence for it. docs/TALK.md has the run.

section.cover(data-timing="15")
  p.a-talk-by a talk by #{authorHandle}
  h1= talkTitle
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Straight in. Every second before 4:00 counts toward the vote.

//- Each thesis slide is a photo with a caption to talk around, credited low on the slide.

//- A beautiful photo, flatly denied. The caption is the yes path's one shout.
section.over-media.is-shout(data-background-image="img/rainbow.jpg" data-timing="15")
  h2 Color doesn't exist.
  p (light has a wavelength. that's all it has.)
  cite.credit Photo: Trougnouf (Benoit Brummer), CC BY 4.0
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Say it flat, over the prettiest rainbow anyone here has seen. No hedging.
      li Visible light is a sliver, about 380 to 700 nanometres, between infrared and ultraviolet.

section.over-media(data-background-image="img/subpixels.jpg" data-timing="20")
  h2 Your eye writes down three numbers.
  p (so your screen only bothers making three)
  cite.credit Photo: Politelyinsulting, CC BY-SA 3.0
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Three kinds of cone squash a whole spectrum into three numbers. Say cones, not rods and cones: rods don't see color.
      li Different spectra with the same three numbers look identical. That's how this screen works, and every screen in the room.

//- Kitaoka's "red strawberry illusion" (sRGB, 2017). No pixel in it has red as its strongest channel, so the aside is literally true. His terms ask for his name and site address with the image.
section.over-media.portrait(data-timing="25")
  img(src="img/strawberries.jpg" alt="A strawberry tart under a strong cyan cast, where the strawberries still look red")
  h2 Your brain makes up the rest.
  p (there isn't a single red pixel in this picture)
  cite.credit Illusion: Akiyoshi Kitaoka, psy.ritsumei.ac.jp/akitaoka
  aside.notes
    ul
      li The thesis. Everything after this is evidence.
      li Say it: every color you've ever named was your head pretending to be the world.
      li If challenged: not one pixel in it has red as its strongest channel. Your brain discounts the cyan light and paints the strawberries red anyway.
      li It's Akiyoshi Kitaoka's red strawberry illusion. His credit is on the slide.
